description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/substrate-gap.ts, strengthen the guard that stops a write from destroying a gap's stored problem statement.

This is one insertion into one file. The anchor below appears exactly once in the file.

Find this exact line:

  gap.created_at = cleanTs(existing.created_at, gap.created_at ?? now);

The next line after it is the existing summary guard, which reads:

  if (typeof gap.summary !== "string" || gap.summary.length === 0) gap.summary = existing.summary;

Immediately after that summary-guard line, insert the following block at the same indentation:

  // A COLLAPSING SUMMARY IS AN ECHO, NOT A REWRITE. The guard above only catches an
  // ABSENT summary. A closing or edit goal is generated as `Close substrate gap <id>:
  // <summary, truncated>` and the writer echoes that goal text back as the new summary,
  // so the incoming value is a LEADING FRAGMENT of the text it destroys. Observed three
  // times on 2026-09-05 against operator gaps: 4781 chars -> 11, 4519 -> 62, 3300 -> 123,
  // each a case-insensitive prefix of what it replaced, taking the gap's falsifier and
  // measurements with it. Because the store REPLACES rather than merges, that erases the
  // very evidence by which a false closure could be detected.
  if (
    typeof gap.summary === "string" &&
    typeof existing.summary === "string" &&
    gap.summary.trim().length > 0 &&
    gap.summary.trim().length < existing.summary.trim().length &&
    existing.summary.trim().toLowerCase().startsWith(gap.summary.trim().toLowerCase())
  ) {
    gap.summary = existing.summary;
  }

Do not modify the anchor line, do not modify the existing summary-guard line, and do not touch any other guard in the function. This is purely an additional condition.

WHY THIS IS NOT A COSMETIC CHANGE: it changes which value is persisted. Today a write whose summary is a short leading fragment of the stored summary overwrites and destroys the longer text; after this change the stored text is retained instead. The existing guard cannot do this because it only tests for an empty or non-string summary, and all three observed destructions passed a non-empty string.

WHY A PREFIX TEST RATHER THAN A LENGTH RATIO: a genuine rewrite by an operator or drafter is essentially never a strict prefix of the text it replaces, so the false-positive surface is very small. A shorter-but-different summary is still allowed through unchanged, which keeps deliberate rewriting and shortening working.

